ibe — provenance note
======================

Kept as a module-local NOTICE (same placement as the sibling
`tlock`/`bls12_381`/`bbs`/`bulletproofs` modules — self-contained
crypto modules not folded into the root `NOTICE`).

Provenance
----------

**Academic reference**: Boneh, D. and Franklin, M., "Identity-Based
Encryption from the Weil Pairing", CRYPTO 2001 (the "FullIdent"
construction, §4.2) — a public academic paper, not a copyrightable
implementation (merger doctrine, `CONVENTIONS.md` §5). This module
implements that construction directly, generalized to an arbitrary
caller-run Private Key Generator rather than any specific deployment.

**Internal design reference**: this module's `encrypt`/`decrypt`
assembly (the FO-transform structure, the `fp12Pow` constant-time `Gt`
exponentiation helper, and the `Ciphertext` `(U, V, W)` shape) is
adapted directly from this repository's own `modules/tlock` (Boneh-
Franklin IBE specialized to a drand randomness beacon PKG, itself
transcribed from `drand/tlock`/`drand/kyber`'s Go source — see
`tlock/NOTICE` for that chain's full third-party provenance). No new
attribution obligation arises from this internal reuse: `tlock`'s own
NOTICE already covers the third-party design references its shape was
originally drawn from, and this module's OWN hash/DST layer
(`ciphersuite.zig`) and Gt-representation handling (no drand-style
cube — see `SPEC.md`'s "No drand-style cube") are original to this
module, not copied from `tlock` or any external source.

`bls12_381` (this repo — `modules/bls12_381`) supplies every field/
group/pairing/hash-to-curve primitive this module builds on (`g1`/`g2`
point arithmetic and wire codecs, `hash_to_curve.hashToCurveG1`,
`pairing.pairing`, `Fr` including `Fr.random`/`Fr.reduceWide`); no new
field, group, or hash-to-curve primitive is implemented here.

No third-party source code (beyond this repository's own `tlock`
module, covered above) was consulted or copied anywhere in this
module.

Test-only third-party DATA (drand interop anchor)
-------------------------------------------------

`src/kat_test.zig` section 5 contains six frozen hex constants — a
BLS12-381 `G2` public key, a `G1` round signature, a 128-byte BF-IBE
ciphertext, a 16-byte plaintext and a 16-byte `sigma` pad — copied
verbatim from `modules/tlock/src/kat_test.zig` section 3, where they
have lived since 2026-07-16. Their origin chain (a `tle`-armored age
fixture from `github.com/drand/tlock` commit
`7ceb44a598293f10c43d2291df9e669c4251fe24`, plus a round signature
fetched live from the quicknet-t beacon) is recorded in full in
`modules/tlock/NOTICE`, which is the authoritative record for them;
nothing new is fetched, and nothing is redistributed here that this
repository was not already distributing.

Licence posture: these are NUMERIC OUTPUT of a public cryptographic
specification (Boneh-Franklin FullIdent over BLS12-381, drand's
`bls-unchained-g1-rfc9380` scheme) produced by running a third-party
implementation — not source code, not a translation of source code,
and not a creative work. No drand or `kilic/bls12-381` source was read
or transcribed for this module: the drand-side ciphersuite section 5
drives is `modules/tlock`'s, imported as a test-only dependency
(`build.zig`'s `test_deps`), so this module adds no new third-party
provenance chain of its own. The published `ibe` module contains none
of this — it is test data only, and `zig build check-testonly` proves
the `tlock` import never reaches the module a consumer imports.

Why the bytes are copied rather than imported: `tlock`'s constants are
private to its own test file, and `modules/tlock/` is shared with
other consumers, so exporting them would mean changing a module this
work had no mandate to change. The copy is guarded by its own
consistency test (the pairing identity binding public key, round
signature and identity together), so a transcription error fails
loudly instead of quietly weakening the anchor.

**Status: REAL.** `ciphersuite.zig`, `ibe.Ciphertext`'s byte codec,
`ibe.setup`/`ibe.extract`, and `ibe.encrypt`/`ibe.decrypt` (the BF-IBE
FullIdent core) are all implemented and tested — see `SPEC.md`'s "KAT
plan" for the verification chain: the encrypt/decrypt ASSEMBLY is
pinned byte-exactly against a genuine drand-Go-produced ciphertext by
instantiating `ibe.Scheme` with drand's ciphersuite, while this
module's own DSTs/tags/no-cube choices are pinned by round-trip,
pairing-consistency and soundness/CCA-rejection tests and cannot be
anchored externally at all (RFC 9380 §3.1 requires each application to
choose its own DST — there is no foreign value for one of ours to
match).
